---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { tools, verticals } from '../lib/data';

// Date of the last edit to this page's text (2026-10-08: referral rewards named beside
// commissions; third-party estimate sentence replaced. 2026-10-05: pricing benchmark reports
// named in the opening, the green box and the money section; report ledger; "Who is
// accountable" rewritten for a one-person operation. Earlier, 2026-10-01: Fit Score
// scope, flag standard, corrections wording). Set to the deploy date when this ships.
const lastUpdated = 'October 8, 2026';
const toolCount = tools.length;
const verticalCount = verticals.length;
// Software tools with a tracked affiliate link (same test as the methodology ledger).
const activeAffiliateCount = tools.filter((t) => t.affiliate_url.startsWith('http')).length;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Editorial Standards', url: '/editorial-standards/' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Editorial Standards & How We Research | WrenchStack"
  description="How WrenchStack researches, verifies, and publishes: our sourcing, fact-checking, independence, reputation-flag, and corrections standards. Vendors cannot pay for scores, rankings or softer reviews."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 prose prose-slate">
    <div class="not-prose mb-8">
      <p class="mb-2 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-orange-600">How we work</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl m-0">Editorial Standards</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-500">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      WrenchStack is an independent research publication covering the software and services that trades
      and field-service businesses buy. We are not a software vendor or a lead broker, and our listings,
      scores and rankings are not for sale. These are the standards every page on this site is held to. If a page falls short of them, email
      <a href="mailto:hello@wrenchstack.com">hello@wrenchstack.com</a> with its URL.
    </p>

    <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border-2 border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 p-5">
      <p class="m-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-emerald-900">
        <strong>The one rule everything else serves:</strong> a vendor cannot pay us for a higher score,
        a better ranking, inclusion in a "best of" list, a softer review, or the removal of a reputation
        warning. We may earn an affiliate commission or a referral reward on some sign-ups, and we sell pricing benchmark reports
        to software vendors. Commissions and referral rewards do not change what we publish, and whether a vendor bought a report
        is not an input to any listing, Fit Score, ranking, award or reputation flag, or to whether a product
        is listed.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>How we research</h2>
    <p>
      Every vendor goes through the same documented process before it's published, regardless of whether
      we have a commercial relationship with it:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Identify.</strong> Vendors enter the directory through our own research or a vendor's submission, not by paying for placement.</li>
      <li><strong>Collect from primary sources.</strong> Pricing comes from the vendor's own public pricing page where it has one; where a figure comes from another source, such as HomeAdvisor's membership fee from the FTC, the entry says so; features and integrations from the vendor's own product pages and documentation.</li>
      <li><strong>Check reputation at the source.</strong> Reputation warnings come from regulator orders, court records, BBB profiles and vendors' own terms, not from forum sentiment. We do not show third-party review scores: software tool pages link to G2 and Capterra, and lead-generation, insurance, payroll and marketing-agency pages link to BBB and Trustpilot.</li>
      <li><strong>Score consistently.</strong> Every field-service software tool is scored with the same published <a href="/methodology/">WrenchStack Fit Score™</a> formula and weights. Lead generation, insurance, payroll and marketing agencies are grouped into tiers by published rules that use verified facts only; the other categories are listed without a score or tier.</li>
      <li><strong>Write and review.</strong> Each review is drafted, checked against the standards on this page, and published with a verification date.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The full step-by-step timeline and the scoring weights are documented on our
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>, which is versioned and carries a public changelog.
    </p>

    <h2>How we verify</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pricing is dated.</strong> Every US vendor entry carries a <em>verified date</em>, and software tool pages mark a price verified more than 90 days ago with an amber or red freshness marker.</li>
      <li><strong>Primary sources win.</strong> When a vendor's claim contradicts its own public page, we publish what the public page says until the page changes.</li>
      <li><strong>No fabricated signals.</strong> We do not invent review counts, ratings, or testimonials. Where we have no first-party reviews for a tool, we say so rather than manufacture them.</li>
      <li><strong>Quote-only pricing is labeled.</strong> When a software vendor publishes no price, the listing says "Custom quote"; in the other categories the listing says in words that the price is quoted or not published. Pricing notes carry no third-party price estimates.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Our sources</h2>
    <p>
      Primary sources are vendors' own pricing pages, product pages, documentation and terms.
      Third-party sources for reputation include the FTC, CFPB, SEC, state attorneys
      general and insurance regulators, court records and BBB profiles. Tool pages link to G2 and Capterra reviews, but we do not copy
      their scores or use them in rankings. When we attach a
      reputation warning to a vendor, we cite the documented basis for it; if we cannot read the source,
      we do not publish the warning.
    </p>

    <h2>How reputation flags work</h2>
    <p>
      Some vendors carry a reputation warning. Each one must trace to a <strong>source we could read</strong>:
      a regulator order or settlement (for example, from the FTC, CFPB, SEC or a state attorney general),
      a court record, the vendor's BBB profile, or the vendor's own terms (for example, a contract that is
      hard to exit). The warning names its source. A warning that rested only on forum posts, or on
      complaint text we could not read, is removed. Whether a vendor pays us does not enter into it,
      and vendors that pay us nothing are listed on the same terms as everyone else.
    </p>

    <h2>Independence and how we make money</h2>
    <p>
      When you sign up with some vendors through our links, we may earn a commission or a referral reward: of the {toolCount}
      software tools, {activeAffiliateCount} have a tracked affiliate or referral link. Commissions and referral rewards do
      not influence what we publish: the same Fit Score formula (for software), the same tier rules, the
      same reputation standards and the same editorial scrutiny apply to every vendor, affiliate partner
      or not. See our
      <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure page</a> and the per-vendor affiliate ledger on the
      <a href="/methodology/#affiliate-ledger">methodology page</a>.
      We also sell pricing benchmark reports to software vendors. Every buyer is listed in the
      <a href="/methodology/#report-ledger">report ledger</a> with its Fit Score, rank, award and reputation
      flag when it ordered and today. Whether a vendor bought a report is not an input to any listing, Fit
      Score, ranking, award or reputation flag, or to whether a product is listed.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If we get something wrong (a price, a feature, a claim), we correct it once the error is confirmed
      against a primary source. Spotted one? Email
      <a href="mailto:hello@wrenchstack.com">hello@wrenchstack.com</a> with the page URL, what's wrong
      and a primary source if you have one.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is accountable</h2>
    <p>
      WrenchStack is run by one person, who does the research, the scoring, the vendor correspondence and
      the sale of pricing benchmark reports. Because those roles are not held by different people, the
      safeguards are in the method: the Fit Score is a published formula applied to vendors' own public
      pages; every report buyer is listed in the <a href="/methodology/#report-ledger">report ledger</a> with
      its Fit Score, rank, award and reputation flag when it ordered and today; and no report is quoted,
      invoiced or sold to a vendor while an evaluation, correction, pricing question, dispute or
      reputation-flag review with it is open, or within 30 days after the latest verdict, correction or
      reputation-flag email we sent it. Editorial questions and accountability, including any claim that a
      report sale affected a listing, go to <a href="mailto:hello@wrenchstack.com">hello@wrenchstack.com</a>.
      Learn more <a href="/about/">about WrenchStack</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping {toolCount} tools across {verticalCount} trades current</h2>
    <p>
      Coverage at this scale only stays useful if it stays fresh. We use data tooling and automation to
      track pricing and structure information across the directory, and verification runs to re-verify
      pricing and re-evaluate reputation flags. Each US entry shows its latest verification date on its
      own page, and the <a href="/verification-log/">verification log</a> counts US entries by the month
      they were last verified. The standards on this page
      govern what gets published. The tooling helps us hold the line at scale, it doesn't replace the
      judgment.
    </p>

    <div class="not-prose mt-10 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-5 text-sm text-slate-600">
      Related: <a href="/methodology/" class="font-medium text-slate-900 hover:underline">WrenchStack Fit Score™ Methodology</a>
      · <a href="/disclosure/" class="font-medium text-slate-900 hover:underline">Disclosure</a>
      · <a href="/about/" class="font-medium text-slate-900 hover:underline">About</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
